Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["TInvar", "Title",
 CellID->1679945197,ExpressionUUID->"9ae1c3d7-ab2b-4259-aa29-184e7579ec6d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The TInvar package provides a set of functions for canonicalization of \
expressions involving the Riemann tensor. The core xAct canonicalization \
functionality provided by ToCanonical is able to efficiently handle \
single-term symmetries such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["R", "TI"], 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["b", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["c", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["d", "TI"]}]], "\[LongEqual]", "-", 
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["R", "TI"], 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox["b", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["a", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["c", "TI"], 
            StyleBox["d", "TI"]}]]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "R_{abcd} = -R_{bacd}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "aeee4689-f4b1-4401-8cf5-11ef42f181ed"],
 ". However the Riemann tensor has additional ",
 StyleBox["multi-term",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " symmetries through the Bianchi identities, and ToCanonical does not have \
any way of handling those and using them to canonicalize expressions. The \
purpose of TInvar is to provide that extra functionality through an new \
function ",
 ButtonBox["RiemannSimplify",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:TInvar/ref/RiemannSimplify"],
 " that makes use of the Bianchi identities to simplify expressions into a \
fully canonical form."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.932611750687072*^9, 3.932611758419495*^9}, {
  3.943351927007386*^9, 3.943351929278243*^9}, {3.9433519963034887`*^9, 
  3.943352023449808*^9}, {3.94335228829916*^9, 3.943352575415922*^9}, {
  3.9433530183165197`*^9, 3.943353064758296*^9}, {3.943353176836444*^9, 
  3.9433531770489597`*^9}},
 CellID->1871418870,ExpressionUUID->"b07576cf-764d-4487-b29a-a4d62d4b1820"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "When considering the problem of canonicalization, one must think about a \
basis with which to represent an expression in its canonical form. Such a \
basis was proposed in \"",
 ButtonBox["Normal forms for tensor polynomials: I. The Riemann tensor",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://dx.doi.org/10.1088/0264-9381/9/5/003"], None}],
 "\", S. A. Fulling, R. C. King, B. G. Wybourne and C. J. Cummins, Classical \
and Quantum Gravity 9, 1151. In that article the authors identified an \
appropriate basis of independent monomials for the Riemann tensor. The \
elements of their basis are organised by order (number of derivatives of the \
metric), degree (number of Riemann tensors) and rank (number of free \
indices). In the article they enumerated all basis elements up to order 12, \
degree 6 and rank 12. TInvar is designed to take an arbitrary Riemann \
polynomial and simplify it into its normal form with respect to that basis."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.94335257788021*^9, 3.94335259460921*^9}, {
  3.943352638234782*^9, 3.943352723923225*^9}, {3.9433528830228*^9, 
  3.9433529441721087`*^9}, {3.943353083242464*^9, 3.9433530925553293`*^9}, {
  3.9433531410626497`*^9, 3.943353150164481*^9}, {3.943353229386243*^9, 
  3.943353412957654*^9}},
 CellID->833104061,ExpressionUUID->"34aa5141-ab5b-4ba3-9d4d-44a33bea4626"],

Cell["\<\
This tutorial covers some basic usage of the package, showing how it can \
verify some well-known identities. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.932611750687072*^9, 3.932611758419495*^9}, {
   3.943351927007386*^9, 3.943351929278243*^9}, {3.9433519963034887`*^9, 
